nd":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"68.7","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"as85EPeASA"}],"key":"HxlD2yfcvi"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"82.6","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"cUD7QdP2IU"}],"key":"UQJ7bYHCqP"}],"key":"kJjZoLbQyv"}],"key":"W1TFhbT5bo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When looking at the graph, the similarities and differences between the two distributions are striking. Both distributions have roughly the same center (medians are 61.4 for Pittsburgh, and 62.7 for San Francisco). However, the temperatures in Pittsburgh have a much larger variability than the temperatures in San Francisco (Range: 49 vs. 12. ","key":"gSPeBXBvN2"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Interquartile Range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"IQR","key":"KxWxrw9XQ6"}],"key":"XejuQTfUr9"},{"type":"text","value":": 36.5 vs. 5).","key":"tc682TL3lM"}],"key":"QyZOwG0GYn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The practical interpretation of the results we got is that the weather in San Francisco is much more consistent than the weather in Pittsburgh, which varies a lot during the year. Also, because the temperatures in San Francisco vary so little during the year, knowing that the median temperature is around 63 is actually very informative. On the other hand, knowing that the median temperature in Pittsburgh is around 61 is practically useless, since temperatures vary so much during the year, and can get much warmer or much colder.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"x5PDzy61AC"}],"key":"HaUPNXkYnY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that this example provides more intuition about variability by interpreting small variability as consistency, and large variability as lack of consistency. Also, through this example we learned that the center of the distribution is more meaningful as a typical value for the distribution when there is little variability (or, as statisticians say, little “noise”) around it. When there is large variability, the center loses its practical meaning as a typical value.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"P3QimEX1yb"}],"key":"czHpdZXy93"}],"class":"tip","key":"T6XkFajADS"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Comparing Groups with Boxplots","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"JT52jSufXA"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-comparing-groups-with-boxplots","label":"Check Your Understanding: Comparing Groups with Boxplots","html_id":"check-your-understanding-comparing-groups-with-boxplots","implicit":true,"key":"WqP6jm9SbP"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-73eca877a522c5138994c2b9b27d024e.mjs","model":{"question":"Two bus routes both have a median travel time of 30 minutes. Route A's IQR is 4 minutes; Route B's IQR is 22 minutes. Which statement gives the best practical interpretation?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Route A is more consistent, so its median is a more meaningful \"typical\" travel time","feedback":"Correct! With small variability, Route A's typical time of 30 minutes is dependable; Route B's identical median tells you much less because times vary widely."},{"correct":false,"text":"Route A is usually much faster than Route B","feedback":"The medians are equal, so on a typical day the routes take about the same time—the difference is in consistency, not center."},{"correct":false,"text":"Route B is more reliable because its IQR is larger","feedback":"A larger IQR means less consistency, not more."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Think of the San Francisco vs. Pittsburgh temperature example—small variability means consistency.","explanation":""},"key":"eDJVlTAci7"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-73eca877a522c5138994c2b9b27d024e.mjs","model":{"question":"Using the side-by-side boxplots of Oscar winners' ages, which comparison is supported by the display?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Actresses tend to win at younger ages than actors","feedback":"Correct! The actresses' entire box (up to Q3 = 42) lies below the actors' median (43.5), showing that actresses tend to win at younger ages."},{"correct":false,"text":"The median age of actors is lower than that of actresses","feedback":"The actors' median (43.5) is well above the actresses' median (34.5)."},{"correct":false,"text":"The actors' ages cover a wider overall range than the actresses' ages","feedback":"The full range is larger for actresses (59 years) than for actors (45 years)."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Compare the positions of the two boxes and medians.","explanation":""},"key":"FqpzuzTRzr"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s Summarize","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":81,"column":1}},"key":"MvCS8apyZb"}],"identifier":"lets-summarize","label":"Let’s Summarize","html_id":"lets-summarize","implicit":true,"key":"lkQSvsVikO"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The five-number summary of a distribution consists of the median (M), the two quartiles (Q1, Q3) and the extremes (min, Max).","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"T8mFRCkmoQ"}],"key":"RcYXSBSaSL"}],"key":"EYO9aSp66j"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":84,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The five-number summary provides a complete numerical description of a distribution. The median describes the center, and the extremes (which give the range) and the quartiles (which give the ","key":"IPZCpnhfrf"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Interquartile Range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"IQR","key":"i1xnEcltoP"}],"key":"vrZvTCnaeV"},{"type":"text","value":") describe the spread.","key":"xulyrV4Jud"}],"key":"Z5kR98iXKC"}],"key":"XrKyCfiABP"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The boxplot graphically represents the distribution of a quantitative variable by visually displaying the five-number summary and any observation that was classified as a suspected outlier using the 1.5(","key":"Epu6Cv9GbW"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Interquartile Range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"IQR","key":"D53ZetsG2Q"}],"key":"MXfdQ3i8B5"},{"type":"text","value":") criterion.","key":"actGhSRdLo"}],"key":"cRcwVSr9Lq"}],"key":"pCXIwgqFJZ"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boxplots are most useful when presented side-by-side to compare and contrast distributions from two or more groups.","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"jCzpS3uFUq"}],"key":"bKbOYzQQ9W"}],"key":"i56GH22d3c"}],"key":"A6jPqrOiMw"}],"key":"R3XOSvExRp"}],"key":"bMh8aqJiwJ"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}